4:["$","$Le",null,{"post":{"slug":"morning-mobility-routine","category":"MORNING MOBILITY","title":"A simple morning mobility routine","description":"Use a short morning mobility routine for hips, shoulders and the spine without turning your morning into a full workout.","lead":"Morning mobility should help you feel ready to move, not leave you exhausted before the day starts.","keywords":["morning mobility routine","morning stretch","daily mobility","mobility exercises"],"stage":11,"activity":"mobility","takeaway":"A few useful movements done often can matter more than a long routine you rarely repeat.","sections":[{"heading":"Start gently","body":"Your first movements do not need to reach your maximum range. Use controlled circles, easy reaches and comfortable positions to gradually explore how your body feels that morning."},{"heading":"Cover the areas you actually use","body":"A practical routine might include the upper back and shoulders, hips and ankles. Pick a few movements that match your training and daily activities instead of trying to stretch every possible area."},{"heading":"Keep it short enough to repeat","body":"Five to ten minutes can be plenty. The routine becomes more useful when it fits naturally before work, school or your first training session."},{"heading":"Use pain as a stop signal","body":"Mobility should feel controlled rather than sharp or alarming. If a movement causes unusual pain, stop and consider guidance from a qualified health professional when appropriate."}]},"index":10,"total":14,"prevHref":"/blog/beginner-cardio-at-home","nextHref":"/blog/rest-day-active-recovery"}]
